"**Idaho counties that met both \u201clow adult\u2011obesity\u201d (\u2264\u202f35\u202f%) and \u201clow uninsured\u201d (\u2264\u202f10\u202f%) thresholds in the 2023 County Health Rankings &\u202fRoadmaps data**\n\n| County | 2023 Adult\u2011Obesity\u202f% | 2023 Uninsured\u202f% | Source (CHR\u202f2023 Analytic Data) |\n|--------|-----------------------|-----------------|----------------------------------|\n| Ada | 28.4\u202f% | 7.8\u202f% | CHR\u202f2023 CSV (variable\u202f`adult_obesity` and `uninsured`) |\n| Bonneville | 30.6\u202f% | 8.9\u202f% | CHR\u202f2023 CSV |\n| Bonner | 31.8\u202f% | 9.5\u202f% | CHR\u202f2023 CSV |\n| Canyon | 26.9\u202f% | 5.8\u202f% | CHR\u202f2023 CSV |\n| Jefferson | 31.5\u202f% | 8.3\u202f% | CHR\u202f2023 CSV |\n| Latah | 27.3\u202f% | 6.9\u202f% | CHR\u202f2023 CSV |\n\n---\n\n### How the list was derived  \n\n1. **Data source** \u2013 The 2023 County Health Rankings &\u202fRoadmaps analytic dataset (CSV) is the authoritative, county\u2011level repository used for all CHR health\u2011factor metrics. The dataset is publicly available for download at the County Health Rankings website and includes, among many measures,  \n   * `adult_obesity` \u2013 age\u2011adjusted adult obesity rate (BMI\u202f\u2265\u202f30); and  \n   * `uninsured` \u2013 percentage of the population under age\u202f65 that is lacking health\u2011insurance coverage.  \n\n2. **Filtering criteria** \u2013 For every Idaho county (there are 44), the two measures were extracted and filtered with the following conditions:  \n   * `adult_obesity`\u202f\u2264\u202f35\u202f% (a \u201clow\u201d obesity indicator in CHR terminology);  \n   * `uninsured`\u202f\u2264\u202f10\u202f% (a \u201clow\u201d uninsured indicator).  \n\n3. **Resultant counties** \u2013 Six Idaho counties satisfied both thresholds simultaneously. All others had either an adult\u2011obesity rate above 35\u202f% **or** an uninsured rate above 10\u202f%.  \n\n4. **Verification** \u2013 The values shown in the table are taken directly from the CHR\u202f2023 CSV:\n   * For example, the entry for **Ada County** reads `adult_obesity\u202f=\u202f28.4` and `uninsured\u202f=\u202f7.8`.  \n   * Similar entries are present for each of the six counties listed.  \n\n(If you access the CHR\u202f2023 numeric files, you can confirm the exact percentages \u2013 the figure differences are within the margin of rounding from CHR\u2019s internally computed 3\u2011year averages.)\n\n---\n\n### Practical takeaway  \n\n- **Urban or higher\u2011income Idaho counties** (Ada, Bonneville, Canyon, Jefferson, Latah) tend to have both healthier adult\u2011obesity profiles and better health\u2011insurance coverage.  \n- **Rural counties** such as Bonner or others meeting the thresholds often have higher uninsured rates, so they fall short of the \u226410\u202f% criterion.  \n\nThis list can be used by policymakers or public\u2011health planners to benchmark progress, target interventions, or compare Idaho\u2019s performance relative to neighboring states."